{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the son of an Israelite woman went out, etc.</b> Scripture did not specify the name of either of them, to indicate that both were blemished — for anyone who goes out to quarrel is surely blemished and is not among the people known by name for fame and glory, but rather among the lowly, men of worthlessness, sons of no name. They had nothing but the name of their paternal lineage: this one was 'the son of an Israelite woman' and this one 'a man of Israel.' Therefore it says: ״וַיִּנָּצוּ בַּמַּחֲנֶה״ — 'they strove in the camp,' for both were instigators of strife and quarrel, and guilt rolled about through the agency of a guilty party: for through the fact that this Israelite entered into a quarrel with him, he caused him to blaspheme the honored Name. And some proof for this is from what is said: ״וְאֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל תְּדַבֵּר לֵאמֹר אִישׁ אִישׁ יְקַלֵּל אֱלֹהָיו״ — 'and speak to the children of Israel, saying: any man who curses his Elohim' etc. — and yet how did He insert into this utterance the statement ״וְאִישׁ כִּי יַכֶּה כָל נֶפֶשׁ אָדָם״ — 'and a man who strikes any human being,' and likewise the verse ״וְאִישׁ כִּי יִתֵּן מוּם בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״ — 'and a man who inflicts a blemish upon his fellow' etc.? And furthermore, this entire verse is superfluous, for behold it is stated explicitly afterwards: ״שֶׁבֶר תַּחַת שֶׁבֶר״ — 'fracture for fracture' etc." "Rather, all of this speaks in disparagement of the instigator and the quarrel, that no peace comes forth from within quarrel, and through quarrel it will come about that one strikes the life of a person or an animal and causes it harm — whether in body or in property. And because the Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Kiddushin 70a): 'anyone who disqualifies with his own blemish, disqualifies' — therefore it says ״וְאִישׁ כִּי יִתֵּן מוּם בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״ — 'and a man who inflicts a blemish upon his fellow' — to cast a blemish upon the consecrated things, as he did with the winking of his lips, such that 'as he did shall be done to him' — for they will say to him that he disqualified with his blemish; and below it speaks of an actual blemish, therefore it says ״כֵּן יִנָּתֶן בּוֹ״ — 'so shall be imposed upon him.' And so too here: this one was the son of an Israelite woman, and this one a man of Israel — for each one disqualified his fellow with a blemish, for neither of them were men of name, and they were known only from the side of their fathers." "And what is written: <b>״וַיֵּצֵא בֶן אִשָּׁה יִשְׂרָאֵלִית וְהוּא בֶן אִישׁ מִצְרִי״</b> — 'And the son of an Israelite woman went out, and he was the son of an Egyptian man' — he is the Egyptian whom Moses killed with the explicit Name. And therefore all the days he would blaspheme within his heart, and the matter was not known outside; but 'all who profane the Name of Heaven in secret are punished from him openly' (Avot 4:5). Therefore HaShem brought it about that he went out from his tent into the midst of the children of Israel, so that his wickedness would be revealed in the congregation — for such is the measure regarding profanation of the Name. And it may be that for this reason it says ״וַיֵּצֵא״ — 'and he went out' — for concerning Moses it says ״וַיֵּצֵא מֹשֶׁה אֶל אֶחָיו״ (Exodus 2:11); so too he says: I too will go out to blaspheme. Concerning Moses it says (Exodus 2:12) ״וַיִּפֶן כֹּה וָכֹה וַיַּרְא כִּי אֵין אִישׁ״ — 'and he turned this way and that and saw that there was no man' — and he acted in concealment; I will go out into the midst of the children of Israel to act in public."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:30.237634237Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}